---
import Layout from "@components/Layout.astro";
import PageHeader from "@components/PageHeader.astro";
import { Card } from "webcoreui/astro";
import DecklistTooltip from "@components/DecklistTooltip.astro";
---

<Layout
    title="Sideboard Strategy & Principles - Replenish Guide"
    description="Master competitive Premodern sideboard theory. Learn how to execute transformative midrange beatdown pivots, execute precise card trimming heuristics, and sculpt your optimal 15-card toolkit."
>
    <PageHeader
        title="Sideboard Strategy & Principles"
        description="Mastering competitive post-board dynamics requires transformative role assessment, strategic trimming heuristics, and 15-card sculpting methodology."
        imageSrc="/src/assets/banners/mage.webp"
        imageAlt="Meddling Mage"
        imageCaption="Meddling Mage by Christopher Moeller"
        isCard={true}
    />

    <Card title="The Sculpting Methodology" titleTag="h2">
        <p style="margin-top: 0;">
            A disciplined tournament pilot relies on a systematic methodology
            before every regional or competitive championship to finalize their
            75. Rather than constructing a sideboard from scratch on tournament
            morning, begin with a master pool of 85–90 proven cards, then
            analytically carve away options that do not align with expected
            local metagame concentrations.
        </p>
        <ol style="padding-left: 1.25rem;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
                <strong>Start Broad:</strong> Maintain an expansive candidate pool
                of 85–90 cards, encompassing archetype variants and specific bullet
                interaction.
            </li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
                <strong>Assess the Metagame:</strong> Heavy red and aggressive tribal
                fields demand sweeping board control (<a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Wrath of God">Wrath of God</a
                >, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Sphere of Law"
                    >Sphere of Law</a
                >). Permission-saturated blue tables necessitate mana taxing (<a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Defense Grid">Defense Grid</a
                >) and robust midrange threat structures.
            </li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
                <strong>Prioritize Utility Slots:</strong> Rank flex options by standalone
                power level and multi-matchup application.
            </li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
                <strong>Cut Strategically:</strong> Eliminate targeted bullets that
                fail to actively serve across at least two major matchup profiles.
            </li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
                <strong>Finalize Transformative Packages:</strong> Deploy post-board
                pivot strategies only when they invert undesirable Game 1 dynamics
                across multiple foundational archetypes.
            </li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 0;">
                <strong>Main vs. Side Positioning:</strong> Assign final positions
                based on mathematical expected matchup frequencies.
            </li>
        </ol>
        <p style="margin-top: 1.5rem; margin-bottom: 0;">
            <strong>The Creature Sideboard Package:</strong> Incorporating <a
                href="#"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Exalted Angel">Exalted Angel</a
            > and <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Meddling Mage"
                >Meddling Mage</a
            > enables an aggressive, high-efficiency midrange transformation after
            sideboarding. This places opponents in an untenable structural bind: if
            they retain heavy anti-enchantment and graveyard hate, they succumb quickly
            to aerial beatdowns; if they dilute their hate to respect creatures, they
            are overrun by the namesake combo.
        </p>
    </Card>

    <Card title="Strategic Transformation & Role Assessment" titleTag="h2">
        <h3 style="margin-top: 0;">Transformation Against Control</h3>
        <p>
            Against heavy blue permission archetypes, the deck seamlessly
            transitions its primary identity. Velocity engines like <a
                href="#"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Frantic Search">Frantic Search</a
            >, reactive lock pieces like <a
                href="#"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Parallax Wave">Parallax Wave</a
            >, and excess spot removal exit the 75. In their place, <a
                href="#"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Defense Grid">Defense Grid</a
            >, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Exalted Angel"
                >Exalted Angel</a
            >, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Abeyance"
                >Abeyance</a
            >, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Arcane Denial"
                >Arcane Denial</a
            >, and full rosters of <a
                href="#"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Meddling Mage">Meddling Mage</a
            > take over. Operating as a proactive midrange beatdown deck completely
            invalidates traditional control post-board logic.
        </p>

        <h3 style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">
            Maintaining the Combo Against Aggression
        </h3>
        <p>
            When pairing against linear aggressive builds, the primary combo
            engine remains completely intact to facilitate an explosive race.
            Swap situational interaction slots; specifically, trim <a
                href="#"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Seal of Cleansing">Seal of Cleansing</a
            > whenever opponents lack relevant enchantment or artifact targets.
        </p>

        <h3 style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">
            Retiring Mages Against Green and Midrange
        </h3>
        <p style="margin-bottom: 0;">
            Against heavy green midrange formations and attrition suites (e.g.,
            The Rock, RG Ponza, Terrageddon), <a
                href="#"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Meddling Mage">Meddling Mage</a
            > exits the formation. Opposing creature curves consistently outclass
            a 2/2 body, and their strategy rarely bottlenecks upon a single named
            spell. Transition into <a
                href="#"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Exalted Angel">Exalted Angel</a
            > to dominate combat math and life-point races.
        </p>
    </Card>

    <Card title="Key Slot Heuristics & Trimming Rules" titleTag="h2">
        <h3 style="margin-top: 0;">Exalted Angel is the Universal Default</h3>
        <p>
            <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Exalted Angel"
                >Exalted Angel</a
            > enters against virtually every non-combo opponent: aggro, control, and
            midrange alike. It stays out exclusively against opposing fast combo shells
            (Enchantress, mirrors, Elves), where diluting your internal combination
            velocity is fatal. When facing an unfamiliar non-combo list, bringing
            in Angel is universally strategically sound.
        </p>

        <h3 style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">Frantic Search is the First Trim</h3>
        <p>
            <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Frantic Search"
                >Frantic Search</a
            > is consistently trimmed against dedicated blue control and discard-heavy
            architectures where inherent card disadvantage (-1 card on resolution)
            proves overly taxing. Conversely, retain full sets against rapid aggro
            and combo where turn-three execution velocity is paramount.
        </p>

        <h3 style="margin-top: 2.5rem; margin-bottom: 0.75rem;">
            Core Trimming Rules Post-Board
        </h3>
        <ul style="padding-left: 1.25rem; margin-bottom: 0;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 0.75rem;">
                <strong>Against Blue Control:</strong> Trim <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Intuition">Intuition</a
                > and <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Frantic Search">Frantic Search</a
                > to eliminate exploitative 2-for-1 counter trades.
            </li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 0.75rem;">
                <strong>Against Non-Creature Shells:</strong> Reduce <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Swords to Plowshares"
                    >Swords to Plowshares</a
                > counts. However, exercise vigilance—many control pilots introduce
                creature clocks (<a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Meddling Mage">Meddling Mage</a
                >, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Exalted Angel"
                    >Exalted Angel</a
                >) post-board, making it mathematically optimal to retain 1 or 2
                removal copies.
            </li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 0;">
                <strong>Against Red Blast Suites (Sligh, Ponza):</strong> Trim expensive
                or card-disadvantaged blue engines like <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Intuition">Intuition</a
                > and <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Frantic Search">Frantic Search</a
                > to deny opponents devastating tempo trades against one-mana blasts
                like <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Pyroblast"
                    >Pyroblast</a
                > or Red Elemental Blast.
            </li>
        </ul>
    </Card>
    <DecklistTooltip />
</Layout>
